3.3 Challenges in the Existing Process

The existing process presented several operational and technical limitations.

3.3.1 No Standardized Incremental Processing

Daily data changes were not processed using a consistent framework. Identifying inserted, updated, and deleted records required additional effort and increased processing complexity.

3.3.2 Limited Historical Tracking

Historical versions of records were not maintained in a structured manner. Once records were updated, previous values were no longer available for auditing or historical reporting.

3.3.3 No Current-State Snapshot Management

There was no dedicated mechanism to maintain a consolidated snapshot representing the latest version of each business record.

3.3.4 Manual Failure Recovery

Pipeline failures required manual investigation and rerunning processes, increasing operational effort and delaying data availability.

3.3.5 Limited Operational Visibility

Execution details such as processing status, record counts, execution duration, and failure reasons were not centrally captured, making monitoring and troubleshooting more difficult.

3.3.6 Sequential Processing

Tables were processed sequentially, resulting in longer execution times as the number of source tables increased.

3.3.7 No Automated Backfill Support

If a daily execution was missed, processing historical dates required manual intervention and increased the risk of incomplete data.

3.4 Limitations Summary
Area	Existing Limitation
Incremental Processing	No standardized change detection
Historical Data	Previous versions not preserved
Snapshot	No latest-state maintenance
Logging	Limited execution visibility
Recovery	Manual reruns required
Performance	Sequential execution
Scalability	Difficult to onboard additional tables
Monitoring	Limited operational metrics
Backfill	Manual processing
